The original cover dates this examination to 22 June 2015, 9.30–11.00am. The archive stores it under `2014/T/a13752w1.pdf`; the cover determines the year. Source: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/ba_computer_science_past_papers/response/2577745/attach/2/202402%20183%20CS%20Papers.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[released examination archive].

The intact copy in https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/maths_papers_2/response/1850333/attach/3/FOI%2020210708%201%20Data.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[the Mathematics Part A release], member `FOI-20210708-1 - Data/a13752w1 (4).pdf`, restores the previously missing printed page 3 and supplies a complete readable counterpart. Questions 1–2 had already been recovered and answered; those answers and all answers to questions 5–8 are retained. The solutions now cover all eight questions and their 37 leaf subparts. The earlier source provenance above is unchanged.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

The <binomial theorem> isolates the only terms that can survive reduction modulo the <prime number> $p$:
$$
(a+b)^p=a^p+b^p+\sum_{r=1}^{p-1}\binom pr a^{p-r}b^r.
$$
For $1\le r\le p-1$, neither $r!$ nor $(p-r)!$ is divisible by $p$, whereas $p!$ is. Consequently $p\mid\binom pr$, and every intermediate term vanishes in <modular arithmetic>. Thus
$$
\boxed{(a+b)^p\equiv a^p+b^p\pmod p.}
$$
This works for arbitrary <integers> $a,b$, including those divisible by $p$. It is the additive identity behind the <Frobenius endomorphism> in <characteristic> $p$.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

For an odd <prime number> $p$, <Euler's criterion> states that
$$
\boxed{a^{(p-1)/2}\equiv\left(\frac ap\right)\pmod p,}
$$
where the <Legendre symbol> is zero if $p\mid a$, one if $a$ is a nonzero <quadratic residue>, and minus one otherwise. Including the zero case makes this formulation valid for every <integer> $a$.

Apply <Euler's criterion> to $a$, $b$, and $ab$. Multiplication in <modular arithmetic> gives
$$
\left(\frac{ab}p\right)\equiv (ab)^{(p-1)/2}
\equiv a^{(p-1)/2}b^{(p-1)/2}
\equiv\left(\frac ap\right)\left(\frac bp\right)\pmod p.
$$
If $p$ divides either factor, both sides are zero. Otherwise both sides belong to $\{-1,1\}$; these two values are distinct modulo the odd <prime number> $p$. The congruence is therefore an equality of <integers>, proving <multiplicativity of the Legendre symbol>:
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{ab}p\right)=\left(\frac ap\right)\left(\frac bp\right).}
$$

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

For distinct odd <prime numbers> $p,q$, the law of <quadratic reciprocity> is
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac pq\right)\left(\frac qp\right)=(-1)^{(p-1)(q-1)/4}.}
$$
Thus the two <Legendre symbols> agree unless both <prime numbers> are congruent to $3$ modulo $4$, in which case they have opposite signs.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

In <Pollard's p-1 algorithm>, a large exponent containing many small factors makes $a^M-1$ divisible by a <prime factor> whose predecessor divides $M$, by <Fermat's little theorem>. A <greatest common divisor> with the number being factored can then separate that <prime factor> from the others.

Choose base $a=2$ and build factorial exponents using <modular exponentiation>. The successive exponents $2!$, $3!$, and $4!$ give
$$
2^2\equiv4,\qquad 2^6\equiv64,\qquad
2^{24}\equiv64^4\equiv35\pmod{1003}.
$$
The first two <greatest common divisors> are $\gcd(3,1003)=\gcd(63,1003)=1$. At the next step the <Euclidean algorithm> gives
$$
\gcd(2^{24}-1,1003)=\gcd(34,1003)=17,
\qquad 1003=29\cdot34+17.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{17\text{ is a factor of }1003,\qquad1003=17\cdot59.}
$$
Here the <element order> of $2$ modulo $17$ is $8$, which divides $24$, even though $17-1=16$ does not divide $24$. The algorithm only needs the <element order> to divide the chosen exponent; divisibility by the full predecessor is a sufficient condition, not a necessary one.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

First simplify the specified residue by <modular exponentiation>. Modulo the <prime number> $113$,
$$
102^2\equiv8,\quad102^4\equiv64,\quad102^8\equiv28,\quad
102^{16}\equiv106,\quad102^{32}\equiv49,\quad102^{64}\equiv28.
$$
Therefore $102^{70}\equiv28\cdot64\cdot8\equiv98$, so the required equation becomes $x^{37}\equiv97\pmod{113}$. Its right side is nonzero, so any solution is a <unit> modulo $113$.

By <Fermat's little theorem>, exponents of such <units> can be reduced modulo $112$. Since
$$
37\cdot109=1+36\cdot112,
$$
raising to the $109$th power is the inverse of raising to the $37$th power. There is therefore exactly one residue class of solutions:
$$
x\equiv97^{109}\equiv97^{-3}\pmod{113}.
$$
Now $97\equiv-16$ and $97^3\equiv-4096\equiv85$. Since $4\cdot85\equiv1$, we obtain
$$
\boxed{x\equiv4\pmod{113}.}
$$
As a direct check, $4^4\equiv30$, $4^8\equiv109$, $4^{16}\equiv16$, and $4^{32}\equiv30$, whence $4^{37}\equiv30\cdot30\cdot4\equiv97$.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

The defining relation $yx=x^4y$ lets us move every occurrence of $y$ to the right of every occurrence of $x$. More generally, repeated <conjugation> gives
$$
yx^r=x^{4r}y,\qquad y^jx^r=x^{4^jr}y^j.
$$
Negative powers cause no problem because $x^{-1}=x^8$ and $y^{-1}=y^2$. Thus every word in the <group presentation> can be put in the required form, reducing powers using $x^9=y^3=e$:
$$
\boxed{g=x^iy^j,\qquad0\le i\le8,\quad0\le j\le2.}
$$
At this stage existence is proved; uniqueness will follow from the concrete <semidirect product> in part (b). Moving $x^r$ past $y^j$ gives the multiplication law
$$
\boxed{(x^iy^j)(x^ry^s)=x^{\,i+4^jr\bmod9}y^{\,j+s\bmod3}.}
$$
The action is consistent with reduction of $j$ modulo $3$ because $4^3\equiv1\pmod9$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For the given <group homomorphism> $\varphi:H\to\operatorname{Aut}(N)$, the external <semidirect product> has underlying <Cartesian product> $N\times H$ and operation
$$
\boxed{(n,h)(m,k)=\bigl(n\varphi(h)(m),hk\bigr).}
$$
The notation $\operatorname{Aut}(N)$ denotes the <automorphism group> of $N$. Closure follows because $\varphi(h)$ is an <automorphism> of $N$. To check <associativity>, calculate both bracketings:
$$
\begin{aligned}
((n,h)(m,k))(r,l)
&=\bigl(n\varphi(h)(m)\varphi(hk)(r),hkl\bigr),\\
(n,h)((m,k)(r,l))
&=\bigl(n\varphi(h)(m\varphi(k)(r)),hkl\bigr)\\
&=\bigl(n\varphi(h)(m)\varphi(h)\varphi(k)(r),hkl\bigr).
\end{aligned}
$$
They agree because $\varphi(hk)=\varphi(h)\varphi(k)$. The identity is $(e_N,e_H)$, using $\varphi(e_H)=\mathrm{id}_N$, and the two-sided inverse is
$$
(n,h)^{-1}=\bigl(\varphi(h^{-1})(n^{-1}),h^{-1}\bigr).
$$
Indeed multiplication in either order gives the identity, using $\varphi(h)\varphi(h^{-1})=\mathrm{id}_N$. This verifies the <group axioms>.

For the specified <group presentation>, take $N=C_9=\langle X\rangle$ and $H=C_3=\langle Y\rangle$. The rule $\alpha(X)=X^4$ defines an <automorphism> because $4$ is coprime to $9$. Furthermore $4^3\equiv1\pmod9$, so $Y\mapsto\alpha$ defines the required <group homomorphism> $C_3\to\operatorname{Aut}(C_9)$. In the resulting <semidirect product> $S$, the canonical generators satisfy
$$
X^9=Y^3=e,\qquad YXY^{-1}=X^4.
$$
Consequently the <group presentation> defines a surjective <group homomorphism> $G\to S$, sending $x$ to $X$ and $y$ to $Y$. The underlying <Cartesian product> gives $|S|=27$, whereas part (a) gives $|G|\le27$. Surjectivity forces equality, so the map is an <isomorphism>:
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_9\rtimes_{\alpha}C_3,\qquad\alpha(X)=X^4,\qquad|G|=27.}
$$
This also proves that the $27$ normal forms in part (a) are distinct and that $x$ has <element order> exactly $9$.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

The <cyclic subgroup> $\langle x^3\rangle$ is normal: <conjugation> by $y$ sends $x^3$ to $x^{12}=x^3$, and <conjugation> by $x$ fixes it. Modulo this <subgroup>, the relation $yxy^{-1}=x^4$ becomes $yxy^{-1}=x$, so the <quotient group> is <abelian>. Thus the <commutator subgroup> $G'$ is contained in $\langle x^3\rangle$.

Conversely the displayed relation gives the <commutator> $yxy^{-1}x^{-1}=x^3$, so $\langle x^3\rangle\subseteq G'$. Hence
$$
G'=\langle x^3\rangle\cong C_3,\qquad G''=\{e\}.
$$
Since $x$ has <element order> $9$, $x^3\ne e$, so the first <commutator subgroup> is nontrivial. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G\text{ is solvable, with derived length }2.}
$$
Here <derived length> counts the number of steps needed for the <derived series> to reach the trivial <group>.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

The given <unitriangular group of degree three over F3> has $27$ elements, just as $G$ does, so counting alone cannot distinguish them. Instead compare <element orders>.

Write an element as $I+U$, with $U$ strictly upper triangular of size $3$. Then $U^3=0$. Over the <finite field> $\mathbb F_3$, the <binomial theorem for commuting matrices> gives
$$
(I+U)^3=I+3U+3U^2+U^3=I.
$$
Every nonidentity element of $H$ consequently has <element order> $3$. In contrast, $x\in G$ has <element order> $9$, by part (b). An <isomorphism> preserves <element order>, so
$$
\boxed{H\not\cong G.}
$$
Equivalently, the <exponent of a finite group> is $3$ for $H$ and $9$ for $G$.

== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Use the <cross-ratio> convention compatible with the permutation identities in this paper. Represent a <projective point> $P_i\in\mathbb{CP}^1$ by a nonzero vector $p_i\in\mathbb C^2$, and write $[p_i,p_j]=\det(p_i,p_j)$. Define
$$
\boxed{(P_0P_1:P_2P_3)=\frac{[p_0,p_2][p_1,p_3]}{[p_0,p_3][p_1,p_2]}.}
$$
The denominator is nonzero because the four <projective points> are distinct. Rescaling any one representative multiplies numerator and denominator by the same nonzero <scalar>, so the definition is independent of the chosen <homogeneous coordinates>. In finite affine coordinates it reads
$$
(z_0z_1:z_2z_3)=\frac{(z_0-z_2)(z_1-z_3)}{(z_0-z_3)(z_1-z_2)},
$$
with the <point at infinity of the complex projective line> handled by the homogeneous formula.

A <projective transformation> is represented by an <invertible matrix> $A\in\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb C)$. Since $[Ap_i,Ap_j]=\det(A)[p_i,p_j]$, the two factors of $\det(A)$ in the numerator cancel the two in the denominator. Therefore **every <projective transformation> preserves this <cross-ratio>**. This also shows that the formula descends from matrices to the <projective linear group>, whose representatives differ by a nonzero common <scalar>.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

The needed general-position theorem is **sharp triple transitivity of the <projective linear group> on the <projective line>**: for any two ordered triples of distinct <projective points>, exactly one <projective transformation> takes the first triple to the second.

In particular, let $T_P$ take $(P_0,P_1,P_2)$ to $(\infty,0,1)$. In these coordinates,
$$
(\infty\,0:1\,z)=z,
$$
so <cross-ratio> invariance gives $T_P(P_3)=\lambda$, where $\lambda=(P_0P_1:P_2P_3)$. Distinctness implies $\lambda\ne0,1$ and $\lambda$ is finite. The entire ordered quadruple is thus normalized to
$$
\boxed{(\infty,0,1,\lambda).}
$$
For a second quadruple $(Q_0,Q_1,Q_2,Q_3)$ with the same <cross-ratio>, its normalizing map $T_Q$ yields the same quadruple. Consequently
$$
\boxed{T_Q^{-1}\circ T_P(P_i)=Q_i\quad(0\leq i\leq3).}
$$
This proves the equivalence. Uniqueness on the first three points also shows that the projectivity carrying one ordered quadruple to the other is unique. The <cross-ratio normalization of four projective points> therefore gives a complete invariant for this ordered classification.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Choose three distinct <projective points> $A,B,C$. Their images under the <injective> map $\tau$ are distinct. Sharp triple transitivity gives a <projective transformation> $S$ agreeing with $\tau$ on these three points. The map $\sigma=S^{-1}\circ\tau$ fixes $A,B,C$ and preserves their <cross-ratios>.

For any other <projective point> $P$, invariance gives
$$
(AB:C\,\sigma(P))=(AB:CP).
$$
Let $T$ normalize $(A,B,C)$ to $(\infty,0,1)$. By part (b), the left side is $T(\sigma(P))$ and the right side is $T(P)$. Since $T$ is a <bijection>, $\sigma(P)=P$. It fixes the original three points too, so $\sigma$ is the <identity map> everywhere. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\tau=S\text{ is a projective transformation}.}
$$
No continuity or prior <surjectivity> assumption was needed. This is the rigidity assertion that <cross-ratio preserving maps are projective>.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

By invariance under <projective transformations> and part (b), it is enough to calculate on the normalized quadruple $(\infty,0,1,\lambda)$. Direct evaluation of the <cross-ratio> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
(0\,\infty:\lambda\,1)&=\lambda,&
(1\,\lambda:\infty\,0)&=\lambda,\\
(0\,\infty:1\,\lambda)&=\lambda^{-1},&
(\infty\,1:0\,\lambda)&=1-\lambda.
\end{aligned}
$$
For instance, $(0\,\infty:1\,\lambda)=(0-1)/(0-\lambda)=1/\lambda$, and $(\infty\,1:0\,\lambda)=(1-\lambda)/(1-0)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{(P_0P_1:P_2P_3)=(P_1P_0:P_3P_2)=(P_2P_3:P_0P_1),}
$$
$$
\boxed{(P_0P_1:P_2P_3)=(P_1P_0:P_2P_3)^{-1},}
$$
$$
\boxed{(P_0P_2:P_1P_3)=1-(P_0P_1:P_2P_3).}
$$
All denominators are legitimate because $\lambda\notin\{0,1\}$. The first identity explains why some distinct permutations always give the same <cross-ratio>.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

The <cross-ratio permutation orbit> is generated by the two operations $\lambda\mapsto1/\lambda$ and $\lambda\mapsto1-\lambda$ from part (d). The full list is
$$
\boxed{\lambda,\quad\frac1\lambda,\quad1-\lambda,\quad
\frac1{1-\lambda},\quad\frac{\lambda-1}{\lambda},\quad
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda-1}.}
$$
These are all the possibilities: the four permutations generated by interchanging both pairs and by exchanging the two pairs leave the <cross-ratio> unchanged. They form the <Klein four-group> inside the <symmetric group> on four symbols, leaving at most $24/4=6$ different values; the displayed operations attain all six rational expressions.

There are fewer than six values exactly when a nonidentity operation in this six-element action fixes $\lambda$: if two operations give the same value, composing one with the inverse of the other gives such a fixed point. For real $\lambda\ne0,1$, the fixed-point equations are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda=1/\lambda&\Longrightarrow\lambda=-1,\\
\lambda=1-\lambda&\Longrightarrow\lambda=\tfrac12,\\
\lambda=\lambda/(\lambda-1)&\Longrightarrow\lambda=2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The other two equations reduce to $\lambda^2-\lambda+1=0$, which has no real root. Hence
$$
\boxed{\lambda\in\{-1,\tfrac12,2\}\text{ are exactly the real exceptional values}.}
$$
Each exceptional value has the same three-element <orbit> $\{-1,\tfrac12,2\}$. Every other permitted real value gives six different values.

For the geometric interpretation, put $P_0=[1:0]=\infty$ and $P_i=[x_i:1]$ for $i>0$. Then
$$
\lambda=\frac{x_1-x_3}{x_1-x_2}.
$$
The three exceptional cases become
$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda=-1&\Longleftrightarrow x_1=\tfrac12(x_2+x_3),\\
\lambda=\tfrac12&\Longleftrightarrow x_3=\tfrac12(x_1+x_2),\\
\lambda=2&\Longleftrightarrow x_2=\tfrac12(x_1+x_3).
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus **the three finite points are collinear and equally spaced, with one their midpoint**, even when the $x_i$ are viewed as points of the <complex plane>. Equivalently they form an <arithmetic progression> in some order. These are the <harmonic conjugate points> configurations with the fourth point equal to the <point at infinity of the complex projective line>.

== 4
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

A map $g$ is <differentiable> at $a$ when a <linear map> $Dg_a:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^k$ approximates its increment to first order:
$$
\boxed{g(a+h)=g(a)+Dg_a(h)+o(\|h\|).}
$$
This <Fréchet derivative> is unique, and its matrix in the standard bases is the <Jacobian matrix>. Differentiability on a domain means differentiability at each point there.

A standard sufficient condition for a zero locus to be a <submanifold> is the <regular level set theorem>: if $g$ is $C^1$ on an open domain and $Dg_a$ is <surjective> at every $a\in g^{-1}(0)$, then
$$
\boxed{M=g^{-1}(0)\text{ is an embedded }C^1\text{ submanifold of dimension }n-k.}
$$
For smooth $g$, the <submanifold> is smooth. The full-rank condition is the relevant <constraint qualification>.

Under this regularity hypothesis, its <tangent space> is
$$
\boxed{T_aM=\ker Dg_a.}
$$
Indeed, if a differentiable curve $\gamma$ lies in $M$ and passes through $a$, the <chain rule> gives $Dg_a(\gamma'(0))=0$. Thus all tangent velocities lie in the <kernel of a linear map>. The <rank-nullity theorem> makes this kernel $(n-k)$-dimensional, the same dimension as the <tangent space>, proving equality. In coordinates, solve the homogeneous linear equations given by the <Jacobian matrix>.

The regularity qualification matters: $g(x)=x^2$ has zero set $\{0\}$, a zero-dimensional <submanifold>, but $Dg_0=0$. Its <kernel> is all of $\mathbb R$, whereas its <tangent space> is zero. This is the phenomenon that <singular defining maps can overestimate tangent spaces>.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

At a constrained <critical point>, the directional <derivative> of $f$ vanishes along every admissible tangent velocity:
$$
Df_a(v)=0\qquad(v\in T_aM).
$$
For a regular zero locus, $T_aM=\ker Dg_a$. A <linear functional> vanishing on this kernel lies in the <row space> of $Dg_a$. Equivalently there are <Lagrange multipliers> $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_k$ such that
$$
\boxed{\nabla f(a)=Dg_a^T\lambda
=\sum_{j=1}^k\lambda_j\nabla g_j(a),\qquad g(a)=0.}
$$
Geometrically, $\nabla f$ is orthogonal to the <tangent space>, so it lies in its <orthogonal complement>, spanned by the independent constraint <gradients>. Thus the level set of $f$ has no first-order change in a tangent direction. The equations provide candidates for constrained extrema; they must still be classified and compared, and boundary or singular cases must be treated separately. The condition is necessary for a regular constrained extremum, not a guarantee of a maximum.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

The feasible set is closed and contained in the rectangle
$$
0\leq x\leq p^{1/p},\qquad0\leq y\leq q^{1/q}.
$$
It is therefore <compact>, and the continuous <function> $xy$ attains a global maximum there by the <extreme value theorem>. At the two boundary endpoints where a coordinate is zero, the product is zero. Meanwhile $(1,1)$ is feasible because the <conjugate exponents> satisfy $1/p+1/q=1$, and its product is one. Hence every maximizer has $x,y>0$.

On that positive region the constraint is $C^1$ with nonzero <gradient>. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations are
$$
y=\lambda x^{p-1},\qquad x=\lambda y^{q-1},\qquad
\frac{x^p}{p}+\frac{y^q}{q}=1.
$$
The first two identities imply $xy=\lambda x^p=\lambda y^q$. Since $x,y>0$, the multiplier is nonzero, so $x^p=y^q=r$. The constraint now gives
$$
r\left(\frac1p+\frac1q\right)=1,\qquad r=1.
$$
Thus the sole interior candidate is $(1,1)$. Existence of a maximum and the boundary comparison prove the unique global result
$$
\boxed{\max xy=1,\qquad\text{attained exactly at }x=y=1.}
$$
This <conjugate-power constrained product maximum> is valid for all real $p,q>1$ with $1/p+1/q=1$; no integrality of the exponents is needed.

\Image[2015-young-inequality-constraint.png]
{title=The constrained product reaches one where the two level curves are tangent}
{description=Original illustration for p=3 and q=3/2. The constraint arc meets the product level xy=1 only at (1,1), where both gradients point along (1,1). The red dashed line is their common tangent.}

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Use a <normalization proof of Young's inequality>. For arbitrary $x,y\geq0$, put
$$
A=\frac{x^p}{p}+\frac{y^q}{q}.
$$
If $A=0$, both coordinates are zero and the desired inequality is equality. If $A>0$, define
$$
X=\frac{x}{A^{1/p}},\qquad Y=\frac{y}{A^{1/q}}.
$$
Then $X^p/p+Y^q/q=1$. Part (c) gives $XY\leq1$, and the <conjugate exponent> identity gives $XY=xy/A$. Therefore <Young's inequality for products> is
$$
\boxed{xy\leq\frac{x^p}{p}+\frac{y^q}{q}.}
$$
The unique equality case in part (c) says $X=Y=1$. Translated back, and including the zero case, this gives the sharp condition
$$
\boxed{\text{equality holds exactly when }x^p=y^q.}
$$
Conversely, if both powers equal $r\geq0$, then $xy=r^{1/p+1/q}=r$ and the right side is $r(1/p+1/q)=r$, directly verifying equality.

== 5
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the unilateral <Laplace transform> convention $\overline f(p)=\int_0^\infty e^{-px}f(x)\,dx$. The <Laplace transform of a derivative> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L[f'](p)=p\overline f(p)-f(0).}
$$
The formula holds in the common <Laplace convergence region> under the usual endpoint and growth hypotheses. The initial value is the right-hand value at zero.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

With the same unilateral <Laplace transform> convention, the standard <sine> formula is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L[\sin x](p)=\frac1{p^2+1},\qquad\operatorname{Re}p>0.}
$$

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

The defining evaluation rule of the <Dirac delta> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L[\delta(x-a)](p)=e^{-ap},\qquad a>0.}
$$
The impulse lies strictly inside the integration domain, so there is no endpoint convention to settle. This <Laplace transform> exists for every complex $p$.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $K(p)=\mathcal L[k](p)$ and $F(p)=\mathcal L[f](p)$. Apply the <Laplace transform of a derivative> twice and use the two zero <initial conditions>. The <differential equation> becomes
$$
(p^2+1)F(p)=K(p),\qquad
\boxed{F(p)=\frac{K(p)}{p^2+1}.}
$$
The factor $1/(p^2+1)$ is the <Laplace transform> of $\sin x$. The <convolution theorem for Laplace transforms> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=(\sin*k)(x)=\int_0^x\sin(x-t)k(t)\,dt.}
$$
This is a causal <convolution>: the value at $x$ uses only the forcing up to $x$. For ordinary continuous $k$, direct <differentiation> gives $f'(x)=\int_0^x\cos(x-t)k(t)\,dt$ and $f''(x)=k(x)-f(x)$, verifying both the <differential equation> and the <initial conditions>. For an impulsive $k$, the same formula is interpreted using <distributions>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

An impulse at $a$ shifts the causal <sine> response. Substituting the <Dirac delta> in the <convolution> from part (i), or using the <Laplace transform time-shift rule>, gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=H(x-a)\sin(x-a)=
\begin{cases}0,&0\le x<a,\\\sin(x-a),&x\ge a,\end{cases}}
$$
where $H$ is the <Heaviside step function>. The answer is continuous at $a$, while its first <derivative> jumps from $0$ to $1$. Consequently its second <distributional derivative> contains exactly one unit <Dirac delta> at $a$, giving $f''+f=\delta(x-a)$. Since $a>0$, both <initial conditions> vanish.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Put $G(p)=\mathcal L[J_0](p)$, initially for real $p>0$. Besides the <Laplace transform of a derivative>, use multiplication by the original variable in the form
$$
\mathcal L[xg(x)](p)=-\frac d{dp}\mathcal L[g](p).
$$
Transforming the <Bessel differential equation>, with its given <initial conditions>, yields
$$
-\frac d{dp}\bigl(p^2G-p\bigr)+(pG-1)-G'=0.
$$
The constant terms cancel, leaving $(1+p^2)G'+pG=0$. This first-order <ordinary differential equation> integrates to
$$
G(p)=\frac C{\sqrt{1+p^2}}.
$$
The <initial value theorem for Laplace transforms> gives $\lim_{p\to\infty}pG(p)=J_0(0)=1$, so $C=1$. Thus the <Laplace transform of the Bessel function J0> is
$$
\boxed{\overline{J_0}(p)=\frac1{\sqrt{1+p^2}},\qquad p>0.}
$$
For complex $p$ with $\operatorname{Re}p>0$, choose the analytic square-root branch agreeing with the positive square root on the positive real axis.

For the forcing $k=J_0$, part (b) now gives $F(p)=(1+p^2)^{-3/2}$. On the other hand, the same multiplication rule gives
$$
\mathcal L[xJ_0(x)](p)=-G'(p)=\frac p{(1+p^2)^{3/2}}.
$$
Taking an <integral> from zero divides the <Laplace transform> by $p$. By <uniqueness of the Laplace transform>,
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\int_0^x tJ_0(t)\,dt.}
$$
A direct check also explains the cancellation. This expression has $f'=xJ_0$ and $f''=J_0+xJ_0'$. The <Bessel differential equation> says $(xJ_0')'=-xJ_0$, so integration from zero gives $xJ_0'=-\int_0^x tJ_0(t)\,dt=-f$. Hence $f''+f=J_0$, and both <initial conditions> hold.

== 6
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Use the <chain rule> along a sufficiently smooth <extremal> of the <functional>. For $E=y'F_{y'}-F$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{dE}{dx}
&=y''F_{y'}+y'\frac d{dx}F_{y'}
-F_x-F_yy'-F_{y'}y''\\
&=y'\left(\frac d{dx}F_{y'}-F_y\right)-F_x.
\end{aligned}
$$
The bracket vanishes by the <Euler-Lagrange equation>. When $F$ has no explicit $x$ dependence, $F_x=0$, giving the <Beltrami identity>:
$$
\boxed{y'F_{y'}-F=\text{constant along the extremal}.}
$$

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For the specified <functional>,
$$
F_{y'}=\frac{y'}{y\sqrt{1+y'^2}},\qquad
y'F_{y'}-F=-\frac1{y\sqrt{1+y'^2}}.
$$
Since $y>0$, the <Beltrami identity> gives $y\sqrt{1+y'^2}=R$ for some $R>0$. This suggests a <circular arc>, but it is worth deriving its equation without dividing by $y'$: such division would lose the point where the arc is horizontal.

Substitute the displayed <partial derivative> and $F_y=-\sqrt{1+y'^2}/y^2$ in the full <Euler-Lagrange equation>. Combining terms over the common denominator gives
$$
\frac d{dx}F_{y'}-F_y
=\frac{yy''+1+y'^2}{y^2(1+y'^2)^{3/2}}=0.
$$
Thus $(yy')'=-1$, so $yy'=h-x$ and a second integration gives
$$
(x-h)^2+y^2=R^2.
$$
This establishes the <semicircular extremals of the hyperbolic length functional> and excludes constant $y$, which would satisfy the first-order <Beltrami identity> for a suitable $R$ but not the full <Euler-Lagrange equation>.

The two prescribed endpoints give
$$
(-1-h)^2+4c^2=R^2=(1-h)^2+4d^2.
$$
Subtracting yields $4h=4(d^2-c^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{h=d^2-c^2,\qquad R^2=(1+h)^2+4c^2,\qquad
y(x)=\sqrt{R^2-(x-h)^2}.}
$$
In particular the centre is $\boxed{(d^2-c^2,0)}$. Admissibility requires $c,d>0$, as the prescribed heights must lie in $y>0$. Under that condition the radicand is positive throughout $[-1,1]$: it is a concave <quadratic polynomial> whose two endpoint values are $4c^2$ and $4d^2$. The positive root consequently gives one smooth admissible arc, uniquely determined by the endpoints.

\Image[short-options-2015-semicircular-extremals.png]
{title=Fixed endpoint heights determine the circle centre; a free endpoint height forces a horizontal tangent}

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

When an endpoint's horizontal coordinate is fixed but its height is free, the <natural boundary conditions for a free endpoint> require $F_{y'}=0$ there. In this problem,
$$
\boxed{\left.\frac{y'}{y\sqrt{1+y'^2}}\right|_{\text{free endpoint}}=0,
\qquad\text{equivalently }y'=0.}
$$
Every admissible <extremal> is an upper <circular arc> with centre on $y=0$, by part (b). Its tangent is horizontal precisely above its centre. Thus imposing the <natural boundary conditions for a free endpoint> at $x=1$ forces $h=1$. The fixed height at $x=-1$ gives $R^2=4+4c^2$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sqrt{4(1+c^2)-(x-1)^2},\qquad -1\le x\le1,\quad c>0.}
$$
Its free endpoint height is $y(1)=2\sqrt{1+c^2}$. Positivity on the whole <interval> follows because the radicand is at least $4c^2$.

There can be no solution satisfying the <natural boundary conditions for a free endpoint> at both ends: an upper <circular arc> has only one point with a horizontal tangent, whereas the two endpoints have distinct horizontal coordinates. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{There is no admissible extremal with both endpoint heights free.}}
$$

== 7
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

A <tree> is a nonempty <connected graph> with no <graph cycle>. A <leaf of a tree> is a <vertex> of <vertex degree> one. As usual in this finite <graph theory> problem, <trees> are finite; the assertion would fail for a doubly infinite <graph path>.

Take a <longest path> $v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_m$ in a <tree> with at least two <vertices>. Connectedness gives $m\ge1$. If $v_0$ had a neighbour outside this <graph path>, the path could be extended, contradicting maximal length. If it had a neighbour $v_j$ with $j\ge2$, that <edge> together with the segment $v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_j$ would make a <graph cycle>. Thus its only neighbour is $v_1$, and it is a <leaf of a tree>. The same argument at the other end makes $v_m$ a second, distinct <leaf of a tree>. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{Every finite tree with at least two vertices has at least two leaves.}}
$$

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Deleting a <vertex> and its incident <edge> cannot create a <graph cycle>, so $T\setminus v$ is acyclic. It remains to establish connectedness and nonemptiness.

Since the <leaf of a tree> $v$ has one neighbour, $T$ has at least two <vertices>, so $T\setminus v$ is nonempty. For any two remaining <vertices> $a,b$, take their unique simple <graph path> in $T$. It cannot pass through $v$: an internal <vertex> on such a <graph path> has two incident <edges>, whereas $v$ has <vertex degree> one. Neither endpoint is $v$, so the entire <graph path> remains after deletion. The remaining <graph> is connected and acyclic, giving
$$
\boxed{T\setminus v\text{ is a tree}.}
$$
This includes the two-<vertex> case, where deletion leaves the one-<vertex> <tree>.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

Here each $T_i$ is a nonempty <connected subgraph of a tree>, rather than a descendant-closed data-structure <subtree>. We prove the <Helly property for connected subgraphs of a tree> by <mathematical induction> on the number of <vertices> of $T$.

For a one-<vertex> <tree>, every nonempty $T_i$ contains that <vertex>. For a larger <tree>, choose a <leaf of a tree> $v$ using part (i), and denote its unique neighbour by $w$. If some $T_i$ consists only of $v$, the assumed pairwise intersections force every $T_j$ to contain $v$, and we are done.

Otherwise, remove $v$ from every $T_i$ that contains it, leaving all other $T_i$ unchanged. Any affected $T_i$ has at least two <vertices>; connectedness forces it to contain $w$ and the <edge> $vw$. Thus $v$ is a <leaf of a tree> of $T_i$, and part (ii) shows that its deletion leaves a nonempty <tree>. All reduced $T_i$ are therefore nonempty <connected subgraphs of a tree> $T\setminus v$.

They still intersect pairwise. An original common <vertex> other than $v$ survives. If a pair had only $v$ in common, both members would also contain $w$, by the previous paragraph, contradicting that it was their only common <vertex>. The induction hypothesis now supplies a common <vertex> of the reduced family, which is also common to the original family. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\bigcap_{i=1}^kV(T_i)\ne\varnothing.}
$$

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

A <matching in a graph> is a set of <edges> no two of which share a <vertex>. A cover here means a <vertex cover>: a set $C$ of <vertices> such that every <edge> has at least one endpoint in $C$. It is not an <edge cover>.

<Kőnig's theorem for bipartite matching> states that, in any finite <bipartite graph>, the maximum size of a <matching in a graph> equals the minimum size of a <vertex cover>:
$$
\boxed{\nu(G)=\tau(G).}
$$
Here $\nu$ is the <matching number> and $\tau$ is the <vertex cover number>.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose a <maximum matching> $M$ and a <minimum vertex cover> $C$. By <Kőnig's theorem for bipartite matching>, $|M|=|C|$. Every <edge> of $M$ must have at least one endpoint in $C$, because $C$ is a <vertex cover>. Distinct <edges> of the <matching in a graph> have disjoint endpoints, so these requirements already use at least $|M|$ distinct <vertices> of $C$.

Since $C$ has exactly that many <vertices>, each <edge> of $M$ has exactly one endpoint in $C$, and no <vertex> of $C$ lies outside the endpoints of $M$. Thus selecting the unique endpoint in $C$ from each <edge> of $M$ produces precisely the <vertex cover> $C$:
$$
\boxed{C=\{\text{one chosen endpoint of each edge of }M\}.}
$$
The argument also covers an edgeless <graph>: take both sets empty.

==== iii
{scope}

===== Solution

For every <matching in a graph> $M'$ and every <vertex cover> $C'$, the disjoint <edges> of $M'$ need distinct covering <vertices>. Therefore
$$
|M'|\le|C'|,\qquad\nu(G)\le\tau(G).
$$
Now assume the assertion in part (ii). It supplies a <matching in a graph> $M$ and a <vertex cover> $C$ formed by choosing one endpoint of each <edge> of $M$. Because these <edges> are disjoint, the chosen endpoints are distinct, so $|C|=|M|$. By the definitions of maximum and minimum,
$$
\tau(G)\le|C|=|M|\le\nu(G)\le\tau(G).
$$
Every inequality must be equality, proving <Kőnig's theorem for bipartite matching>:
$$
\boxed{\nu(G)=\tau(G).}
$$

== 8
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Use coordinates $X=(ct,x,y,z)^T$ and <Minkowski metric> $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$. A real $4\times4$ <matrix> $L$ is a <Lorentz transformation> precisely when it preserves the <Minkowski interval> for every $X$, equivalently
$$
\boxed{L^T\eta L=\eta.}
$$
It is proper when $\boxed{\det L=+1}$, and orthochronous when $\boxed{L^0{}_0>0}$, equivalently $L^0{}_0\ge1$. The latter selects the <orthochronous Lorentz group>, which preserves future-directed timelike <four-vectors>. Indeed the time-time entry of the preservation identity gives $(L^0{}_0)^2-\sum_{i=1}^3(L^i{}_0)^2=1$, so $|L^0{}_0|\ge1$. Imposing both conditions gives a <proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation>.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Now use $(ct,x)^T$ and $\eta_2=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$. Write
$$
L=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\d&e\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Preservation of the <Minkowski metric> says that the first column has squared norm $1$, the second has squared norm $-1$, and the columns are orthogonal for the <Minkowski inner product>:
$$
a^2-d^2=1,\qquad b^2-e^2=-1,\qquad ab-de=0.
$$
Orthochronousness gives $a\ge1$. From the orthogonality equation, $b=de/a$; substituting in the second norm identity gives $e^2=a^2$. Hence $(b,e)=\varepsilon(d,a)$ with $\varepsilon=\pm1$. The <determinant> is then $\varepsilon(a^2-d^2)=\varepsilon$, so properness forces $\varepsilon=1$. Thus $b=d$ and $e=a$.

Set $\beta=d/a$. The first norm identity implies $|\beta|<1$ and $a=(1-\beta^2)^{-1/2}$. With $u=c\beta$, this gives
$$
\boxed{L=\begin{pmatrix}\gamma&\gamma u/c\\\gamma u/c&\gamma\end{pmatrix},
\qquad\gamma=(1-u^2/c^2)^{-1/2},\quad |u|<c.}
$$
Conversely, the displayed <matrix> has $\gamma^2-(\gamma u/c)^2=1$, equal off-diagonal entries, <determinant> one, and positive time-time entry. Direct multiplication therefore gives $L^T\eta_2L=\eta_2$, proving both directions. The plus signs are the <Lorentz boost> convention used in the paper; reversing the boost direction replaces $u$ by $-u$.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

The signed <rapidity> corresponding to <velocity> $u$ is
$$
\boxed{\phi=\operatorname{artanh}(u/c)=\frac12\log\frac{1+u/c}{1-u/c}.}
$$
Equivalently $u/c=\tanh\phi$, $\gamma=\cosh\phi$, and $\gamma u/c=\sinh\phi$. Thus the <Lorentz boost> from part (i) becomes
$$
B(\phi)=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh\phi&\sinh\phi\\\sinh\phi&\cosh\phi\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Multiplying two such <matrices> gives diagonal entries $\cosh\phi\cosh\psi+\sinh\phi\sinh\psi=\cosh(\phi+\psi)$ and off-diagonal entries $\cosh\phi\sinh\psi+\sinh\phi\cosh\psi=\sinh(\phi+\psi)$, by the addition identities for <hyperbolic functions>. Hence
$$
\boxed{B(\phi)B(\psi)=B(\phi+\psi).}
$$
The resulting <rapidity> is $\phi+\psi$. In <velocity> variables this is the <relativistic velocity-addition formula> $u\oplus v=(u+v)/(1+uv/c^2)$.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

For $N\ge2$, each adjacent pair of walkways has relative <rapidity> $\phi_N=\operatorname{artanh}(1/N)$. There are $N$ such steps between $P_0$ and $P_N$, so the <equal-rapidity chain of collinear Lorentz boosts> has total <rapidity> $N\phi_N$. Thus the actual <velocity> is
$$
\boxed{v_N=c\tanh\left(N\operatorname{artanh}\frac1N\right)
=c\frac{\left(\frac{N+1}{N-1}\right)^N-1}{\left(\frac{N+1}{N-1}\right)^N+1}.}
$$
It is strictly below the <speed of light> for every finite $N\ge2$. Adding the adjacent <velocities> as ordinary numbers ignores the <relativistic velocity-addition formula>.

For large $N$, the <Taylor series> of the inverse <hyperbolic tangent> gives
$$
N\operatorname{artanh}(1/N)=1+\frac1{3N^2}+O(N^{-4}).
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{v_N\sim c\tanh1=\frac{e^2-1}{e^2+1}\,c\approx0.761594156\,c.}
$$
More precisely, $v_N/c=\tanh1+\operatorname{sech}^2(1)/(3N^2)+O(N^{-4})$, so the limit is approached from above. The case $N=1$ would prescribe an adjacent relative <velocity> equal to the <speed of light>; it does not define an admissible walkway <inertial frame>.
